# Architecture Roadmap (ADM Phase E/F)

The roadmap is the bridge between **Gap Analysis (Phase E)** and **Migration Planning (Phase F)**. It answers: *in what order, and in what groups of work, do we close the identified gaps?*

A roadmap without Transition Architectures is a wish list. A Transition Architecture without a roadmap is an academic exercise. Both are required for a gap that cannot be closed in one step.

## Core concepts

### Transition Architecture

A Transition Architecture is a **named, valid intermediate state** between the Baseline Architecture and the Target Architecture. It must be:
- **Stable** — the system can operate in this state indefinitely if needed; it is not a half-built state.
- **Valuable** — it delivers some of the target value, not just structural groundwork.
- **Named** — use the convention `TA-NN-<kebab-slug>` (e.g. `TA-01-parallel-run`, `TA-02-new-platform-live-legacy-read-only`, `TA-03-full-target`).

**Not every gap needs a Transition Architecture.** Only propose one when:
- The gap is too large to close in a single delivery (typically more than one team, more than one quarter)
- There are hard dependencies that prevent closing all aspects simultaneously
- Risk mitigation requires an intermediate stable state (e.g. a parallel run period)

### Horizons

Horizons are **time bands**, not delivery dates. They communicate priority and sequencing, not contractual commitments.

| Horizon | Typical band | Characteristics |
|---------|-------------|-----------------|
| **H0** | Now → ~6 months | High confidence, well-scoped, decisions made, resources committed |
| **H1** | ~6 → 18 months | Direction clear, dependencies identified, scoping in progress |
| **H2** | ~18 months+ | Strategic intent established, details to be resolved in a future ADM cycle |

H0 work packages should be fully sequenced and scoped. H1 and H2 are indicative — they will be refined as H0 delivers and Phase H feeds the next cycle.

### Work packages

A work package is the unit of delivery within a horizon. Each work package:
- Has a short name and a one-sentence scope statement
- Closes one or more named gaps from the Gap Analysis
- Has at most one team/programme responsible for it (avoid shared ownership)
- Is linked to the Transition Architecture it produces or contributes to

Work packages within a horizon are not necessarily sequential — they may run in parallel if there are no dependencies between them. Dependencies between packages must be named.

## What good looks like

**Gaps drive packages, not the other way round.**
Start from the gap list (Phase E Gap Analysis), not from a list of projects. Every work package must close a named gap. A work package with no gap justification is a project, not architecture.

**Dependencies are explicit.**
If Work Package B cannot start until Work Package A completes, name the dependency. "These packages are roughly in order" is not dependency management.

**Transition Architectures are described, not just named.**
Each TA entry states: what is live, what is not yet live, what data migration state it represents, and what operational capabilities are available. "TA-01 — partial migration" is not a description.

**Phase H feed is planned.**
State what metrics or events will trigger a review of the roadmap. Roadmaps become stale when they are not connected to a change management process. Name the review trigger (e.g. "TA-01 achieved", "H0 delivered", "business priority change").

**The decommissioning plan is part of the roadmap.**
For replacement and consolidation scenarios, the roadmap must show when legacy systems are retired — not just when new ones go live. A roadmap that ends with two systems in production is not complete.

## Roadmap artifact structure

Default location: `ea-practice/ArchitectureRoadmap.md` or `transformation/<programme>/Roadmap.md` (check `AGENTS.md`).

Recommended sections:

```markdown
## Summary
One paragraph: what this roadmap covers, the target it works toward, and the current status.

## Baseline → Target → Gap summary
Brief reference to the Gap Analysis that drives this roadmap.

## Transition Architectures
### TA-01 — <name>
Description of the state, what is live, what is not yet live.

## Roadmap

### H0 — <Date range>
| Work package | Closes gap(s) | Produces | Dependencies | Owner |
|…|

### H1 — <Date range>
| … |

### H2 — Strategic intent
| … |

## Decommissioning schedule
| System | Decommission trigger | Target date | Owner |

## Review triggers
| Trigger | Action |
```

## Phase E → Phase F handoff

Phase E produces the roadmap structure and Transition Architectures. Phase F adds:
- Detailed sequencing within H0 (sprint/quarter level)
- Data migration plans per work package
- Cutover strategies (big-bang vs. parallel vs. phased)
- Resource and cost estimates
- Risk register entries per work package

Do not put Phase F level detail into the roadmap artifact — reference it from the relevant work package implementation plans.

## Boundaries

- Gap identification belongs in Phase E artifacts (Gap Analysis)
- Detailed migration/cutover planning belongs in Phase F artifacts
- The roadmap is the summary bridge — it links gaps to packages to timelines, not the other way round
